Hmm wait, the corrected: `ρ_ret·T_v > (V/N − c_r − I·M)/(κ q I)`? With κ=q=I=1 → 400-10-20=370. But the original §2.5 formula is `κ·q·(ρ_ret·I·T_v + c_r + I·M) > V/N`. So `ρ_ret·I·T_v > V/(Nκq) − c_r − I·M`. With κ=q=1: 370. Right.

But the order says the region is `ρ_ret·T_v > V/N − c_r − I·M`, matching REVISION-DS3 correction (with κ=q=I=1).

Now the order's falsifiable question adds the space-distribution constraint from DS-6: the attacker must recruit the space β_d needed. The gap (grieta) constraint: if β_d ≤ B(ε) then the attacker recruits for free (from keys with zero balance), so the punishment doesn't bite. The DS-6 result says B(ε) ≈ 0.095 (with corrected α_dens) or empirical ~2e-4, well below the threshold `1−2α` for α∈[0.2,0.4]. So the gap is closed: β_d needed to cross the drift is ≈ 1−2α (0.6 for α=0.2? wait). Let's check: β_cruce = 1−2α. For α=0.20 → 0.60; α=0.40 → 0.20. But DS-3's β_d_min for P_obj may be smaller than β_cruce (because even a sub-threshold drift can win with small probability). Indeed for α=0.33, β_d_min ≈ 0.279 for P*=1e-3. And the threshold `1−2α` = 0.34. So the "grieta" is whether B(ε) ≥ β_d_min (free recruiting) rather than β_cruce. Actually in DS-3 they used `β_d ≤ B(ε)` with β_d=0.279 and B(ε)=0.675. So the relevant comparison is B(ε) vs β_d_min (minimum β_d to achieve P_obj).

Wait DS-6 compares B(ε) against `1 − 2·α`. Hmm, but DS-3's actual check is `β_d_min ≤ B(ε)`. Let me re-read REVISION-DS6: "Frente al umbral de DS-3 (`B(ε) > 1 − 2·α_atacante`), la grieta se cierra para α = 0.20/0.25/0.33/0.40". So they use `1−2α` as the comparison, which is conservative (β_d_min ≤ 1−2α typically? Actually β_d_min ≤ β_cruce=1−2α for the same P because to win with prob P you need less than the drift-crossing). So comparing B(ε) < 1−2α is a sufficient condition for the gap closed if we also need β_d_min... hmm, actually if B(ε) < β_d_min then no free recruiting. Since β_d_min ≤ 1−2α, B(ε) < β_d_min is stronger than B(ε) < 1−2α. So DS-6's conclusion "B(ε) << 1−2α" doesn't automatically prove B(ε) < β_d_min. But it's close enough given huge margins (B~1e-4 vs threshold 0.2). Actually the empirical B is ~1e-4 to 1e-3, and β_d_min for P_obj as low as 1e-6 could be small. Hmm.

