### The region and which constraint closes each border

For each (α, V, P*, f, ρ, T_v, R, q_g), check:
- (G) No-free-gap: `B(ε_eff) < β_d` OR the deterrence cost condition. Actually (G) is needed for (a) to be meaningful.
- (A) Deterrence: `C_ret > V` (and/or total).
- (B) Honest: `ε_h·L ≤ 0.01·income`.

The "region" is the set of (ρ_ret, T_v, R_slots, f, q_g) for which (A) and (B) hold, for each (ε_h, α, V, P*). Output tables and CSV.

"Qué restricción la cierra en cada borde": at low `ρ_ret·T_v`, constraint (A) fails (not enough cost to deter). At high `ρ_ret·T_v` (and high q_g, ε_h), constraint (B) fails (honest loss too high). Also `T_v > F` feasibility. So the region is an interval in `ρ_ret·T_v`, closed at the lower border by (A) and at the upper border by (B). I can compute analytically:
- (A): `coef·max(0, β_d − B(ε_eff)) > V`. With `coef = f_conf·ρ_ret·I·λ·T_v^eff/2`.
  - If `β_d ≤ B(ε_eff)`: (A) fails (free gap). Note `B(ε_eff)` depends on `T_v^eff` (smaller x = ε/(λ T_v^eff) → B smaller as T_v grows). So longer T_v reduces the free gap → helps (A).
  - Else: `ρ_ret·T_v^eff > 2V/(f_conf·I·λ·(β_d−B))`.
- (B): `ε_h·[f_conf·(ρ_ret·I·λ·f_h·T_v^eff/2 + q_g) + c_r] ≤ 0.01·λ·f_h·I·T_year`.
  - Solve for T_v: `T_v^eff ≤ [0.01·λ f_h I T_year/ε_h − f_conf q_g − c_r] / (f_conf ρ_ret I λ f_h/2)`.
  
So for fixed ρ_ret, the region in T_v is `(Tv_min_A, Tv_max_B)`, plus `Tv > F`. Nice closed forms. And the border that closes: A at the bottom, B at the top. Also the "no-free-gap" is subsumed in A (if B(ε)≥β_d, A fails for all T_v). 

This is clean and I can produce the region tables.

